# Lid switch — uinput, so a userspace daemon can report SW_LID.
#
# The Gemini has a hall sensor for lid detection: the vendor device tree wires
# it as `hall: hall { compatible = "mediatek, hall-eint" }` on GPIO66, and the
# vendor's drivers/input/hall/hall.c reports SW_LID from it. Mainline has no
# such driver, and the vendor's is EINT-driven — which we cannot use, because
# pinctrl-mt6797 has no EINT support (blockers.md B-11). That is the same
# reason the keyboard matrix runs in polling mode.
#
# So the lid is read by polling GPIO66 from userspace (gemini-lid, in
# device-config/desktop/) and reported through uinput as a real EV_SW/SW_LID
# device, which is what systemd-logind needs in order to apply
# HandleLidSwitch* policy. Module, not built-in: nothing in early boot needs it.
#
# The tidier end state is a `gpio-keys-polled` node in the device tree with
# linux,input-type = <EV_SW> and linux,code = <SW_LID>, which would need no
# userspace at all — but that is a DTB change and therefore a flash, and this
# is not.
CONFIG_INPUT_UINPUT=m
